/P?S MONITOR SYSTEM I/O ROUTINE LOADER

/CORE IMAGE OF BINARY FORMAT FILE

/THIS PAGE IS A GOOD EXAMPLE OF THE SIMILARITY TO CORE IMAGE OF
/THE P?S BINARY FORMAT. THIS CODE IS LOADED VIA /I OPTION BY
/THE BINARY LOADER TO ALLOW DEVICE-INDEPENDENT SOFTWARE TO BE
/WRITTEN. THE P?S FORMAT CONSISTS OF 22 GROUPS OF 7 WORDS PLUS
/TWO OR THREE WORDS (DEPENDING ON HARDWARE) FOR A TOTAL OF 200
/OR 201 WORDS PER BLOCK (STANDARD). EACH BLOCK IS INDEPENDENT
/OF THE OTHER BLOCKS LOGICALLY CONTAINED IN A FILE, SO
/THEORETICALLY THEY COULD BE DISASSOCIATED. THIS /I BLOCK
/CONFORMS TO THIS CONVENTION, AND CONSISTS OF A ONE BLOCK LONG
/FILE INTERNAL TO THE BINARY LOADER ITSELF, I. E. THE BINARY
/LOADER LOADS THIS BLOCK OVER ITSELF CAUSING THE CODE TO READ
/IN THE SYSTEM I/O ROUTINES AND THEN DISAPPEAR.




/BIT CONVENTIONS FOR BINARY FILES

/THE 7 WORD LONG GROUPS CONSIST OF 1 CONTROL WORD AFFECTING THE
/NEXT 6 WORDS, THE CONTROL WORD BEING DIVIDED INTO 6 BIT PAIRS,
/EACH ONE OF WHICH AFFECTS THE ASSOCIATED WORD IN THE 6 WORDS
/IN THE FOLLOWING MANNER:

/00     -       WORD OF CODE;LOAD IT AND ADVANCE ORIGIN
/01     -       END OF FILE;DON'T LOAD THIS WORD
/10     -       ORIGIN SETTING;USE THIS WORD FOR NEW ORIGIN
/11     -       FIELD SETTING;WORD IS A CDF INSTRUCTION;EXECUTE IT

/TYPICAL FILES HAVE A DEFAULT ORIGIN SETTING OF 0200 FOLLOWED
/BY LOADABLE WORDS OF CODE, SO THE FIRST WORD IS 4000 TO
/INDICATE THAT THERE IS ONLY AN ORIGIN SETTING FOLLOWED BY THE
/ORIGIN TO 0200 FOLLOWED BY 5 DATA WORDS. FURTHER LOADING OF
/THE SAME PAGE WOULD HAVE CONTROL WORDS OF 0000 IN THE NORMAL
/CASE, THEREFORE THE TYPICAL FILE IS SLIGHTLY LESS THAN 6/7 OF
/THE EQUIVALENT CORE IMAGE FILE HOWEVER THE FILE HAS NEITHER A
/CHECKSUM NOR IS BLOCK ORIENTED ALLOWING MUCH LATITUDE IN
/PROGRAMMING OF PATCHES TO OTHER PROGRAMS. INDEED, THIS /I
/OPTION IS A PATCH TO THE BINARY LOADER AND COULD BE
/IMPLEMENTED BY A USER PROGRAM.
/BINARY LOADER /I BLOCK

        FIELD   1               /WHERE GENASYS WANTS IT

        *6200                   /WHERE IT GENERATES

        NOPUNCH

IGEN,   FIELD   0               /WHERE IT LOADS

        *7400                   /GENERATION ADDRESS

        ENPUNCH

/       BIT DEFINITIONS FOR BINARY CONTROL WORDS


        DA1=    0000            /DATA IN WORD 1
        DA2=    0000            /DATA IN WORD 2
        DA3=    0000            /DATA IN WORD 3
        DA4=    0000            /DATA IN WORD 4
        DA5=    0000            /DATA IN WORD 5
        DA6=    0000            /DATA IN WORD 6

        DATALL= 0000            /DATA IN ALL WORDS
        
        EF1=    2000            /END OF FILE IN WORD 1
        EF2=    0400            /END OF FILE IN WORD 2
        EF3=    0100            /END OF FILE IN WORD 3
        EF4=    0020            /END OF FILE IN WORD 4
        EF5=    0004            /END OF FILE IN WORD 5
        EF6=    0001            /END OF FILE IN WORD 6
        
        OR1=    4000            /ORIGIN SETTING IN WORD 1
        OR2=    1000            /ORIGIN SETTING IN WORD 2
        OR3=    0200            /ORIGIN SETTING IN WORD 3
        OR4=    0040            /ORIGIN SETTING IN WORD 4
        OR5=    0010            /ORIGIN SETTING IN WORD 5
        OR6=    0002            /ORIGIN SETTING IN WORD 6
        
        FL1=    6000            /FIELD SETTING IN WORD 1
        FL2=    1400            /FIELD SETTING IN WORD 2
        FL3=    0300            /FIELD SETTING IN WORD 3
        FL4=    0060            /FIELD SETTING IN WORD 4
        FL5=    0014            /FIELD SETTING IN WORD 5
        FL6=    0003            /FIELD SETTING IN WORD 6
